1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,935
In the last part of this lesson,

2
00:00:01,935 --> 00:00:04,005
let's talk about pricing strategy.

3
00:00:04,005 --> 00:00:08,535
Setting up the correct price for your product is a very complex affair.

4
00:00:08,535 --> 00:00:12,555
A lot of companies, there are whole departments that deal with this problem.

5
00:00:12,555 --> 00:00:15,930
So what do you need to know about pricing as a product manager?

6
00:00:15,930 --> 00:00:19,545
There are several factors to consider when setting pricing;

7
00:00:19,545 --> 00:00:22,965
what value does the user get from using the product,

8
00:00:22,965 --> 00:00:25,725
what does the product cost to produce,

9
00:00:25,725 --> 00:00:28,485
what is the company's goal for the product,

10
00:00:28,485 --> 00:00:30,780
and what do competitors charge?

11
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:32,910
Let's take them one by one.

12
00:00:32,910 --> 00:00:37,825
Value. Users can get value from our product which saves the money or time,

13
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:39,815
or just makes their life better.

14
00:00:39,815 --> 00:00:42,710
Your price can be higher than the value that

15
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:45,380
the user thinks she's getting from your product,

16
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:47,095
or user just won't buy it.

17
00:00:47,095 --> 00:00:50,360
Cost. Your product may not be sustainable in

18
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:54,290
the long run if you price it below what it cost your company to create it.

19
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:57,410
That being said, there are often strategic reasons

20
00:00:57,410 --> 00:01:00,700
why a company will choose to price a product below cost.

21
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:03,154
Leave the number are good examples.

22
00:01:03,154 --> 00:01:08,300
They strategically subsidize the cost of a right to build new market share. Product goal.

23
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:10,430
It is critical to understand how

24
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:13,715
your product fits into your company's portfolio and vision.

25
00:01:13,715 --> 00:01:17,030
Sometimes, company have products which are free,

26
00:01:17,030 --> 00:01:23,070
and the whole purpose of this product is just for greater good and not to make money.

27
00:01:23,070 --> 00:01:26,475
Google has a lot of products that think in that category.

28
00:01:26,475 --> 00:01:30,635
Or product might be created to help save costs in another part of the portfolio.

29
00:01:30,635 --> 00:01:34,055
We can see this in some of examples from the earlier courses.

30
00:01:34,055 --> 00:01:39,020
I have insurance company like Kaiser Permanente might want to give the purchasers of

31
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:41,330
its insurance a free health tracking app to improve

32
00:01:41,330 --> 00:01:44,425
the user's health and reduce the use of medical services.

33
00:01:44,425 --> 00:01:46,500
Competitors price.

34
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:51,440
Users won't to use your product if they can get a competitor's product for a lower price.

35
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:55,720
You can work around this constraint by providing a superior product,

36
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:57,530
but you still need to be aware of

37
00:01:57,530 --> 00:02:02,345
the competitive marketplace and how your product fits in the user's price value model.

38
00:02:02,345 --> 00:02:04,700
Once I've looked at the value, cost, goals,

39
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:08,420
and competitor's price, we can set a pricing model for our product.

40
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:11,060
There are a lot of different pricing models,

41
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:13,445
and we'll just talk about a few of them here.

42
00:02:13,445 --> 00:02:17,070
Free. You can give your product away for users,

43
00:02:17,070 --> 00:02:19,040
like many of Google's services and

44
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:22,450
the hypothetical Kaiser Permanente app we discussed above.

45
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:25,960
Freemium. The basic product is free,

46
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:28,765
but you can purchase additional features or content.

47
00:02:28,765 --> 00:02:31,315
Dropbox is a good example of this.

48
00:02:31,315 --> 00:02:34,070
You can get a limited amount of storage for free,

49
00:02:34,070 --> 00:02:39,030
but many users opt-out to purchase extra storage. Ad supported.

50
00:02:39,030 --> 00:02:41,680
In this case, the product itself is free,

51
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:45,950
but it generates revenue by renting its users eyeballs to advertisers.

52
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:51,230
A lot of information of websites and apps use this model including weather dot com.

53
00:02:51,230 --> 00:02:55,175
There's also a wide range of paid pricing models.

54
00:02:55,175 --> 00:02:58,040
Value-based pricing. In this case,

55
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:01,370
the price is based on the value of a user gets from the product.

56
00:03:01,370 --> 00:03:04,480
Professional athletes are a good example of this model.

57
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:07,640
LeBron James is worth millions of dollars because millions of

58
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:10,895
fans are willing to pay high price to watch him play,

59
00:03:10,895 --> 00:03:12,985
and wear jerseys of his number.

60
00:03:12,985 --> 00:03:14,615
Cost-based pricing.

61
00:03:14,615 --> 00:03:17,645
There are many different flavors of cost-based pricing,

62
00:03:17,645 --> 00:03:20,750
but they all use the cost of creating the product as

63
00:03:20,750 --> 00:03:24,845
a base and added markup on that base to set the price.

64
00:03:24,845 --> 00:03:26,580
Dynamic pricing.

65
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:27,995
In dynamic pricing,

66
00:03:27,995 --> 00:03:31,870
the price changes reflect changes in the supply or demand.

67
00:03:31,870 --> 00:03:36,150
Ride-sharing apps like Uber do this with search pricing.

68
00:03:36,150 --> 00:03:38,720
Price discrimination. In this case,

69
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:42,085
different users are charged a different price for the same product.

70
00:03:42,085 --> 00:03:45,650
The best example of this is airline ticket pricing.

71
00:03:45,650 --> 00:03:49,280
A last minute traveler will definitely pay higher price for

72
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:53,900
an airline seat than a traveler who's able to plan ahead in the United States.

73
00:03:53,900 --> 00:03:56,200
Outside, it might be the opposite,

74
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,500
and there are so many more models.

75
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:01,670
As a PM, it is not your job to be a pricing expert,

76
00:04:01,670 --> 00:04:05,435
but you do need to understand the basic principles of pricing strategy,

77
00:04:05,435 --> 00:04:08,190
and how your product fits in.

